 THE CAMERA THIS RIDE IS SEEN THROUGH, derived from the rider's state and nothing else.

 `Safari chapter Camera` is the projection; `Safari chapter Lens` is its width. This is
 what those take THIS frame: how far the lens has pulled in, which way the head is turned,
 and the pose the whole scene is drawn from.

 ALL OF IT IS A READ. Nothing here is state and nothing here feeds the simulation -- the
 roll is a deadbanded view of a tilt the rider carries untouched -- which is why it is a
 chapter of its own rather than part of Rider. Split out of Safari on 2026-09-03, where it
 had sat above the drawing and the ride step with nothing in common with either.
 We say:

Section: The Lens Pulls In

 THE LENS PULLS IN -- narrows -- on two independent things, and the frame takes
 the deeper of them. Leaning into a corner narrows it toward 0.35x; watching a
 crossing cat or gawking at the roadside pigs narrows it too. `camera.camFocal`
 is a min over the two, so whichever is more distracted wins.

 THE WHOLE FRAME READS ONE FOCAL. The projection, the backdrop and the sun all
 take it, which is what keeps them one camera; the only place the STABLE base
 focal survives is the critter size cull, and Render's own prose argues that at
 length (culling animals on a leaned focal would pop them in and out of a corner).

  cat-attention : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  cat-attention (w) (s) =
    let seg = list-at w s.segment
    in if seg.has-cat then cat-focus (seg.cat.along - s.along) s.v else 0.0

  ride-focal : List Segment, RiderState -> Real
  ride-focal (w) (s) =
    let lean-frac = real-min (real-abs s.tilt / max-lean) 1.0
    in let attention = real-max (cat-attention w s) (gaze-focus s.focus)
    in cam-focal lean-frac attention

Section: Where He Is Looking

 The extra camera yaw on top of his heading: the distracted gaze toward the pigs
 plus a subtle head-turn into a lean. It swings the WHOLE view, so it folds into
 the scene pose and the backdrop heading alike.

  head-yaw-frac : Real
  head-yaw-frac = 0.15

  view-yaw-for : RiderState -> Real
  view-yaw-for (s) = s.gaze-yaw + head-yaw-frac * s.tilt

  heading-for : RiderState -> Real
  heading-for (s) = s.heading + view-yaw-for s

  pose-for : List Segment, RiderState -> Pose
  pose-for (w) (s) =
    Pose { along = s.along, across = s.across, yaw = s.yaw + view-yaw-for s, hw = (list-at w s.segment).width / 2.0 }

Section: The Canvas Roll

 THE BIKE'S LEAN AS THE CANVAS SHOULD ROLL IT, which is not quite the lean the
 simulation carries. The lean search leaves a residue around 1e-5 rad on a
 straight that never quite reaches zero; below a thousandth of a radian -- under
 half a pixel of bank at the screen edge -- the camera is upright for every
 practical purpose and the blitter's straight-line fast paths key on exactly
 zero. The simulation is untouched: this is a read, not a state.

  roll-deadband : Real
  roll-deadband = 0.001

 THE `let` IS LOAD-BEARING AND IT SHOULD NOT BE. Written as a bare
 `if real-abs s.tilt < roll-deadband then 0.0 else s.tilt`, this function is
 INLINED and never emitted, so the shim -- which is outside the program -- fails
 to link against it. Binding the field first makes it a real function. Measured
 over four variants: the same shape over a scalar parameter emits, and the
 arithmetic-bodied `view-yaw-for` beside it emits. PORTING_NOTES B12.

  rider-roll : RiderState -> Real
  rider-roll (s) =
    let t = s.tilt
    in if real-abs t < roll-deadband then 0.0 else t
